      "outputTokens": 818,
      "costUsd": 0.007422499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here. A thirty thousand dollar car loses value at fifteen percent of its current value each year. Right now, fifteen percent of thirty thousand is four thousand five hundred, so it's losing four thousand five hundred dollars a year. But the rate doesn't stay put. When the car is worth fifteen thousand, fifteen percent of that is two thousand two hundred and fifty, so it's losing value half as fast. Half the value, half the rate. So what curve flattens exactly in step with its height? And what's the car worth after eight years?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-d19/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1341,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.9029999999999916,
        "after": "falls.",
