      "inputTokens": 151,
      "outputTokens": 1175,
      "costUsd": 0.010650499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Now the shapes. Take ay equals two, and change k. With k positive, the curve climbs ever more steeply. Turn k down through zero, and it becomes a flat line, then falls, flattening out as it drops. That decay curve gets closer and closer to the t axis, but never reaches it, because e to any power is positive. So Q equals zero is a horizontal asymptote. For growth, the same thing happens going back in time. Now make A negative. Every value changes sign, so the graph reflects in the t axis. It still obeys d Q d t equals k Q, with the same asymptote. But money or mass can't be negative, so models use a positive A, and t from zero on."
    },
